{
  "id": "T18_37_an_ignore_ends_nothing_under_any_policy_and_a_policy_end_act_citing_one_is_rejected",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.7.4",
    "7.7.5",
    "10.2.9",
    "9.5.6",
    "9.5.5",
    "6.7.7",
    "7.6.10",
    "7.6.14",
    "9.5.7",
    "9.5.8"
  ],
  "description": "A change story and an expectation property governed by the fixture policy, and a conflict story governed by an application policy the reference does not implement, are each ignored. No ignore records an end act, baseline or policy: each unit reads open (outstanding for the property) under its policy, with attention ignored, and what remains omits all three only under the attention filter. A policy end act whose record is an ignore is rejected under the fixture policy, for the change story and for the property, and under the application policy. After the application's policy ends the conflict story at a review, a revise of that policy end act whose content gives the ignore as its record is also rejected. Rejected writes leave nothing: the ignore has no dependents, and the transition check lists only the accepted application end act as unverified.",
  "note": "7.7.4: \"That an ignore ends nothing holds under every policy: a policy may not use an ignore, or any other attention preference (9.5.6), as the cause of an ending, and no policy end act gives an ignore as its record (10.2.9).\" 10.2.9 rejects \"an end act naming a policy ... or such an end act, or a revise of one, whose content gives an ignore as a `record` (7.7.4)\". The rule names any policy, so the fixture policy's explicit end acts (steps 22 and 23) are rejected on that ground even though the reference implements the policy; each rejected write has a cause the policy defines, a target that is a begin act of a unit the policy governs, and no other end act of that policy on its begin act, so the ignore record is its only defect.",
